# Multi-store storefront context

## Product
- Customers sign up on our website and create their own online store. Each store has its own branding, products, and orders.
- One Next.js app serves ALL stores. The current store is resolved from the request host (subdomain or custom domain).
- Backend will be WordPress with WooCommerce (one sub-site per store). It is NOT ready, so build everything against mock data behind a thin data layer. Swapping to WooCommerce later must only touch `lib/api`.
- Purpose right now: a polished stakeholder demo that proves one codebase can power many differently branded stores.
- Scope: home page, product listing, product detail, add to cart, checkout popup. Do NOT build signup, onboarding, or a store admin area.

## Architecture boundaries
- Resolve store id via `lib/get-current-store-id.ts` (host, demo cookie, `NEXT_PUBLIC_DEFAULT_STORE_ID`).
- Theme tokens come from store settings and are applied as CSS variables.
- Components receive data via props or `useStore()`. Only pages, the provider, and the cart store talk to `lib/api`.
- Every API function takes `storeId` as its first argument.
- Cart persistence is per store.
- Keep UI text and comments free of em dashes.
